digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper proposes to develop and evaluate Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 multiprincipal element alloys with increased Mn content in direct energy deposition, testing whether Mn-tuned fcc phase destabilization and in-situ fcc-to-hcp transformation can disrupt columnar grain growth and enable crack-free, grain-refined AM parts, using thermodynamic modeling, operando XRD, and multiscale microstructural analysis to reveal the mechanism.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A coherent AM alloy-design study is supported when the problem of columnar/cracking-prone MPEA microstructures, a composition/phase-stability lever, a concrete Mn-series in DED, and mechanism-revealing characterization tools are jointly established."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) The observed association between increasing Nb content and a columnar-to-equiaxed transition in deposited HEAs motivates testing whether composition-driven phase stability changes or transformation/secondary-phase effects can disrupt epitaxial grain growth."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because varying composition can introduce secondary phases in MPEAs, and phase/transformational effects can alter AM grain structure, composition is a plausible control knob for AM microstructure design."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) A deeper understanding of composition-driven process-structure-property relations in MPEAs would support strategic placement of targeted compositions for improved printed-component performance."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Since adding alloying elements to increase constitutional supercooling is listed among successful AM grain-refinement strategies, alloying for constitutional supercooling is a valid grain-refinement strategy to consider."];
  "R16" [label="(0,0,0) The reported reduction of hot-crack density after in situ Al alloying in a crack-susceptible HEA supports the idea that targeted composition engineering can mitigate cracking risks in some AM alloys."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) Because metastability engineering deliberately reduces phase stability to harness transformation/interface mechanisms, phase-stability tuning is a purposeful alloy-design lever."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) The fact that changing Mn in conventionally processed FeMnCoCr affects fcc/hcp-related stacking-fault-energy behavior makes Mn content a meaningful variable for probing phase behavior in FeMnCoCr alloys."];
  "R20" [label="(0,0,0) Because the grain-refinement mechanism is unclear and difficult to observe during/after solidification, combining thermodynamic modeling, operando XRD, and multiscale microstructural evaluation is well matched to the problem."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Using FeMnCoCr to study composition-phase-stability-process-microstructure-property linkages is an appropriate way to connect composition and phase stability to crack-free, refined AM microstructure outcomes."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) Because DED microstructures tend to be more columnar than PBF under the stated conditions, DED is a demanding and relevant AM context for testing alloy-design-based grain refinement."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) Strong texture and anisotropy from columnar grains limit AM applications, so addressing columnar growth through grain refinement is an important AM objective."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 is studied at x = 40, 45, and 50, the Mn-series provides a suitable experimental matrix for testing how changing one composition variable affects phase-stability-related grain refinement."];
  "R31" [label="(0,0,0) The listed successful AM grain-refinement strategies, including adding alloying elements to increase constitutional supercooling, show that the study’s alloy-design framing is aligned with established AM grain-refinement approaches."];
  "R32" [label="(0,0,0) The defined DED platform, Mn-series, and mechanism-revealing methods support a concrete study plan for evaluating composition-driven grain refinement."];
  "R33" [label="(0,0,0) The DED platform choice together with the defined Mn-series and methodology provides a concrete study instantiation, rather than by itself establishing every broader motivational context element."];
  "R36" [label="(0,0,0) The Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 Mn40/Mn45/Mn50 series concretely specifies the composition-tuning experiment."];
  "R37" [label="(0,0,0) The combination of thermodynamic modeling, operando XRD, and multiscale microstructural evaluation is an appropriate mechanism-revealing methodology for the unclear grain-refinement problem."];
  "R38" [label="(0,0,0) The Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 compositions at x = 40, 45, and 50 specify the composition-tuning portion of the experimental plan."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) When a grain-refinement mechanism is unclear because in situ solidification observations are difficult, combining operando measurements with modeling is a reasonable strategy for resolving the transformation pathway."];
  "R40" [label="(0,0,0) DED is an appropriate demanding platform for testing composition-driven microstructure control because DED microstructures tend to be more columnar under the stated low-scan-speed conditions."];
  "R43" [label="(0,0,0) AM thermal gradients, rapid cooling, and partial remelting can form columnar/textured grains; therefore AM grain refinement toward equiaxed grains is needed to reduce anisotropy and qualification limits."];
  "R44" [label="(0,0,0) Because AM-processed MPEAs can outperform cast parts in strength and corrosion resistance, improving microstructure and defect control in AM MPEAs is a valuable goal."];
  "R45" [label="(0,0,0) Cases where composition modifications are associated with columnar-to-equiaxed transition or reduced hot cracking support composition engineering as a promising AM microstructure and defect-control approach."];
  "R46" [label="(0,0,0) Because brittle intermetallic secondary phases can crack during solidification under AM residual stresses, alloy design should pursue grain refinement while avoiding brittle cracking pathways."];
  "R47" [label="(0,0,0) DED is an appropriate demanding platform to test composition-driven grain refinement because the study targets microstructure control in DED, where microstructures tend to be more columnar than in PBF."];
  "R48" [label="(0,0,0) Secondary phases that pin grain boundaries or disrupt epitaxial grain growth can contribute to AM grain refinement."];
  "R49" [label="(0,0,0) A concrete DED Mn-series study instantiation supports the overall study context for proposing the research idea."];
  "R52" [label="(0,0,0) The association between composition changes and columnar-to-equiaxed transition in deposited HEAs makes composition/phase-stability tuning a plausible approach to investigate for AM grain refinement."];
  "R53" [label="(0,0,0) Because AM alloy design must avoid brittle cracking while targeted alloying can reduce hot-crack density in some HEAs, the approach should aim for crack-free grain refinement."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Evidence that AM-processed MPEAs can outperform cast parts supports treating MPEAs as a promising high-performance materials platform for AM studies, provided microstructure and defect issues are addressed."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because MPEAs are described as a compelling route for fabricating high-performance components by AM, pursuing AM processing routes for MPEAs is scientifically motivated."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) AM thermal gradients, rapid cooling, and partial remelting can produce columnar, highly textured grains; therefore grain refinement toward more equiaxed structures is a central AM microstructure-control goal."];
  "b_alloy_series" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the study uses Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 with x=40/45/50 and samples are labeled Mn40/Mn45/Mn50, therefore a systematic Mn-series experimental matrix is defined."];
  "b_anisotropy" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given columnar grains cause anisotropy and anisotropy is undesirable for performance/qualification, therefore equiaxed grain refinement is a key target."];
  "b_metastability_need" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given a guiding design strategy is needed in vast compositional space and metastability engineering provides such a strategy, therefore metastability/phase-stability concepts are appropriate for alloy design planning."];
  "b_processing_challenge" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given AM processing of MPEAs is challenging and AM induces columnar/textured grains, therefore MPEA AM needs deliberate grain-structure control."];
  "c6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the grain-refinement mechanism is elusive and combining thermodynamic modeling + operando XRD + multiscale microscopy is appropriate, therefore this combined methodology should be used to reveal the mechanism during AM."];
  "ctx3_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If cracking risk exists and composition engineering can mitigate cracking, then the approach should target crack-free refinement."];
  "inst_case" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given DED is a relevant context and a systematic Mn-series with appropriate methodology is defined, therefore the concrete instantiation for the study is present."];
  "inst_rule" [label="(0,0,0) If a platform is relevant and a concrete composition series plus methodology are specified, then a concrete study instantiation exists."];
  "kA1" [label="(0,0,0) If we observe that changing composition alters grain structure in AM, then a plausible explanation is that composition changes solidification-phase stability and triggers phase transformations/secondary phases that interrupt epitaxial columnar growth."];
  "kD1" [label="(0,0,0) If AM produces columnar/textured grains, then mechanical properties become anisotropic and performance/qualification can be limited; therefore grain refinement toward equiaxed grains is desirable."];
  "kD2" [label="(0,0,0) If composition controls phase stability/phase transformations during solidification, then composition can be used to control grain structure (columnar vs equiaxed) in AM."];
  "kD3" [label="(0,0,0) If secondary phases pin grain boundaries or disrupt epitaxial growth, then they can refine grains; however, if they are brittle intermetallics, they can promote cracking under AM residual stress."];
  "kD4" [label="(0,0,0) If a key mechanism is poorly understood because in situ observations are hard, then combining thermodynamic modeling, operando diffraction, and multiscale microscopy is an appropriate strategy to infer the mechanism."];
  "kD5" [label="(0,0,0) If a DED process tends to form more columnar grains than PBF, then DED particularly needs alloy-design-based microstructure control to obtain refined/crack-free builds."];
  "kD6" [label="(0,0,0) If one composition variable can tune phase stability in a chosen MPEA system, then systematically varying that variable across multiple compositions can test how phase stability during solidification affects grain refinement and cracking."];
  "kD7" [label="(0,0,0) If a material class has strong intrinsic properties and is AM-processable, then solving AM microstructure/defect issues can enable high-performance printed components."];
  "kI1" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple AM case studies show grain refinement or crack mitigation after targeted compositional modifications, then composition engineering is an effective lever for AM microstructure/defect control."];
  "kI2" [label="(0,0,0) If a mechanism is uncertain across reports, then systematically combining operando measurements with modeling can reveal spatiotemporal transformation pathways."];
  "n10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, successful AM grain-refinement strategies include mixing pre-existing alloys, adding nucleant particles, and adding alloying elements to increase constitutional supercooling."];
  "n11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, in MPEAs, varying composition to introduce a secondary phase is an effective way to change microstructure."];
  "n12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, secondary phases (bcc/L21/Laves) can pin grain boundaries and disrupt epitaxial grain growth."];
  "n13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, adding elements to create secondary phases may form brittle intermetallics that crack during solidification because they cannot accommodate AM residual stresses."];
  "n14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, the mechanism by which phase transformation causes grain refinement is unclear due to difficulty measuring/interpreting spatiotemporal microstructure/defect evolution during/after solidification."];
  "n16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, the study demonstrates an alloy-design concept for AM using Fe80-xMnxCo10Cr10 (x=40,45,50)."];
  "n18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, in conventionally processed FeMnCoCr, decreasing Mn improves strength and ductility because it lowers the SFE difference between stable fcc and deformation-induced hcp."];
  "n19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, the study instead increases Mn to destabilize primary fcc during solidification, enabling microstructure control in DED where microstructures tend to be more columnar (low scan speed vs PBF)."];
  "n20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, FeMnCoCr is used to study the composition–phase stability–process–microstructure–property linkage to achieve crack-free, refined AM microstructures."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, in AM, MPEAs are a compelling route to fabricate high-performance components."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, AM-processed MPEAs can outperform cast parts in strength and corrosion resistance."];
  "n6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, AM processing of MPEAs is challenging, especially for systems that solidify with preferred crystal orientations."];
  "n7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, AM thermal gradients/rapid cooling/partial remelting can cause columnar grains, producing highly textured microstructures."];
  "n9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, a deeper understanding of composition-driven PSP relations in MPEAs enables strategic placement of targeted compositions to enhance printed-component performance."];
  "plan_case_common" [label="(0,0,0) If multiple consistent premises jointly support a context statement, then it is reasonable to generalize that the context is established."];
  "plan_case_instance" [label="(0,0,0) deduction: Given the DED platform choice and the Mn-series/methodology choices are defined, therefore there exists a concrete study instantiation supporting the overall context."];
  "r10_8" [label="(8,10,8) Currently, columnar grains in AM cause anisotropy in properties."];
  "r10_9" [label="(19,10,9) If alloy design is optimized, then fine equiaxed grains can be achieved in AM."];
  "r14_6" [label="(10,14,6) Currently, strong texture and anisotropy from columnar grains limit AM applications."];
  "r16_22" [label="(10,16,22) If an alloy has high constitutional supercooling, then it can override the negative effect of high thermal gradients and promote equiaxed fine grains in AM."];
  "r17_7" [label="(13,17,7) Currently, in situ Al alloying in a crack-susceptible HEA drastically reduces hot-crack density in a certain composition range."];
  "r1_10" [label="(18,1,10) Currently, a metastability-engineering strategy exists: deliberately reduce phase stability to harness interface/transformation mechanisms for improved mechanical behavior."];
  "r1_21" [label="(18,1,21) If phase stability is decreased, then transformation-induced hardening can occur."];
  "r1_33" [label="(3,1,33) If compositional space is vast, then a guiding design strategy is needed to navigate it effectively."];
  "r22_13" [label="(11,22,13) Currently, the columnar-to-equiaxed transition in deposited HEAs is associated with increasing Nb content."];
